WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.241
In the previous session,
we learned how to organise

2
00:00:03.321 --> 00:00:06.192
our data and summarise
them with a few indicators.

3
00:00:06.964 --> 00:00:09.387
But when a table has hundreds of rows,

4
00:00:09.467 --> 00:00:11.890
it can be hard to understand
what is happening.

5
00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:16.080
So we need other ways to look at

6
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.314
these data, particularly through graphs.

7
00:00:18.394 --> 00:00:20.345
We might ask: are there several groups?

8
00:00:20.425 --> 00:00:23.510
Is there a change over time?
A relationship between two variables?

9
00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:26.057
This is where graphs become useful.

10
00:00:26.137 --> 00:00:29.007
Yet the same data can
produce very different

11
00:00:29.087 --> 00:00:31.957
graphs,
which do not all show the same thing.

12
00:00:32.037 --> 00:00:34.584
In this session, we will learn

13
00:00:34.676 --> 00:00:39.026
to choose a representation that
suits our question, read it,

14
00:00:39.106 --> 00:00:43.856
and avoid choices that could mislead us.

15
00:00:44.308 --> 00:00:47.628
Imagine that each row
of this table represents

16
00:00:47.708 --> 00:00:51.709
an observation made in a catchment
on a particular date.

17
00:00:51.789 --> 00:00:53.854
We have a quality category:

18
00:00:53.934 --> 00:00:56.417
good, very good, moderate, and so on.

19
00:00:56.497 --> 00:00:59.242
We also have a nitrate
concentration in milligrams

20
00:00:59.322 --> 00:01:02.538
per litre, a temperature
and a dissolved oxygen concentration.

21
00:01:02.618 --> 00:01:04.344
Now suppose someone asks:

22
00:01:04.436 --> 00:01:07.792
“What graph should we
make with these data?”

23
00:01:08.084 --> 00:01:11.488
It sounds simple, but first we need

24
00:01:11.568 --> 00:01:15.516
to ask: “What is the graph for?”

25
00:01:15.684 --> 00:01:17.836
We could ask several questions.

26
00:01:17.916 --> 00:01:21.505
For example,
which quality category is most common?

27
00:01:21.585 --> 00:01:25.423
How are nitrate concentrations
distributed spatially?

28
00:01:25.503 --> 00:01:28.894
Do concentrations differ
between catchments?

29
00:01:28.974 --> 00:01:32.564
We could also ask whether
temperature and oxygen are related.

30
00:01:32.644 --> 00:01:34.696
Finally, how does nitrate

31
00:01:34.776 --> 00:01:37.256
concentration change over the months?

32
00:01:37.348 --> 00:01:40.049
That is already five questions
about the same table.

33
00:01:40.129 --> 00:01:43.028
Yet we will not look at the same variables

34
00:01:43.108 --> 00:01:46.008
or use the same kind of visualisation.

35
00:01:46.088 --> 00:01:49.931
The visualisation therefore
depends on our research question.

36
00:01:50.011 --> 00:01:52.066
Let us see how to choose

37
00:01:52.146 --> 00:01:54.450
between these different visualisations.

38
00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:56.536
We will follow this sequence.

39
00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:59.168
First, we formulate our research question

40
00:01:59.248 --> 00:02:02.376
and identify the variables
needed to answer it.

41
00:02:02.948 --> 00:02:07.405
Then we decide what visual task is
needed to represent the variable.

42
00:02:07.485 --> 00:02:12.252
This guides us towards
a suitable type of graph.

43
00:02:12.752 --> 00:02:15.791
Let us return to our initial question:

44
00:02:15.871 --> 00:02:19.411
“Which quality category is most common?”

45
00:02:19.491 --> 00:02:22.530
Here, the variable is
categorical and ordinal.

46
00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:25.760
This means that the quality
categories have an order:

47
00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.715
we can rank them from
“very good” to “poor”.

48
00:02:29.795 --> 00:02:33.972
Our aim is to compare
the different counts.

49
00:02:34.064 --> 00:02:37.178
To do this, we will use a bar chart.

50
00:02:37.258 --> 00:02:40.023
We will explore several
types of graphs throughout

51
00:02:40.103 --> 00:02:42.867
the session so that you can

52
00:02:42.947 --> 00:02:46.191
choose for yourselves.
Here, we use this chart.

53
00:02:46.669 --> 00:02:49.542
We simply want to count

54
00:02:49.622 --> 00:02:52.706
the observations in each category.

55
00:02:52.786 --> 00:02:56.397
We put the counts in this table and check

56
00:02:56.477 --> 00:03:00.141
that they add up to 48,
the original number of observations.

57
00:03:00.221 --> 00:03:02.777
The frequency table already
gives us the answer.

58
00:03:02.857 --> 00:03:06.865
But to compare these numbers more quickly,
we represent them

59
00:03:06.957 --> 00:03:10.931
as lengths. This gives us a bar chart.

60
00:03:11.011 --> 00:03:14.665
Position identifies the category,
and the length

61
00:03:14.745 --> 00:03:19.039
of the bar shows the count,
or absolute frequency.

62
00:03:19.119 --> 00:03:22.240
Since the categories are distinct,
we leave a small

63
00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:25.547
gap between the bars to make
the chart easier to read.

64
00:03:25.627 --> 00:03:28.136
But a categorical variable does not

65
00:03:28.216 --> 00:03:31.181
automatically require this type of chart.

66
00:03:31.261 --> 00:03:34.887
With just three categories,
if we mainly want

67
00:03:34.967 --> 00:03:38.643
the exact values,
a small table will be more effective.

68
00:03:38.723 --> 00:03:41.028
A graph becomes useful when

69
00:03:41.108 --> 00:03:43.697
we want to see a ranking quickly,

70
00:03:43.789 --> 00:03:46.997
so that it stands out at a glance.

71
00:03:47.077 --> 00:03:49.282
The idea is to choose a visualisation

72
00:03:49.362 --> 00:03:51.982
that makes the data easier to read.

73
00:03:52.457 --> 00:03:55.786
Here, there are so few
values that we can even

74
00:03:55.866 --> 00:03:59.410
add the counts to the graph
to complete the information.

75
00:03:59.891 --> 00:04:02.826
Now for the second question: how are

76
00:04:02.906 --> 00:04:06.320
nitrate concentrations distributed?

77
00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:09.143
This time, the variable is
numerical and continuous.

78
00:04:09.223 --> 00:04:12.685
We will not use a bar chart.
Instead, we will

79
00:04:12.765 --> 00:04:16.322
look at its empirical distribution,
introduced in the last video.

80
00:04:16.402 --> 00:04:19.991
We already have a tool for this:
the histogram.

81
00:04:20.083 --> 00:04:22.792
As a reminder,
we divide the range of values

82
00:04:22.872 --> 00:04:26.065
into intervals, also called bins.

83
00:04:26.145 --> 00:04:28.747
We count the observations in each

84
00:04:28.827 --> 00:04:33.011
interval to determine
the height of the bar.

85
00:04:33.498 --> 00:04:36.962
Visually, this resembles a bar chart.

86
00:04:37.042 --> 00:04:40.307
But here, the rectangles touch because

87
00:04:40.387 --> 00:04:44.300
the intervals cover a continuous scale.
Bar width

88
00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:48.044
has a meaning:
it represents the width of the interval.

89
00:04:48.124 --> 00:04:52.137
For example, from 2 to 3 mg/L of nitrates,
then from 3 to 4.

90
00:04:52.217 --> 00:04:54.733
In a bar chart, we need

91
00:04:54.813 --> 00:04:57.758
to preserve the order
of the categories if the variable

92
00:04:57.850 --> 00:05:00.415
is ordinal. However,

93
00:05:00.495 --> 00:05:03.172
the bars do not need to touch.

94
00:05:03.252 --> 00:05:06.886
Although the two graphs look similar,
this is what distinguishes them.

95
00:05:06.966 --> 00:05:10.262
With bars, we count observations

96
00:05:10.342 --> 00:05:13.189
by category. With a histogram, we examine

97
00:05:13.269 --> 00:05:17.095
how numerical values are distributed
along a continuous scale.

98
00:05:17.577 --> 00:05:20.903
As we explained in session

99
00:05:20.983 --> 00:05:24.643
2, the number of histogram bins

100
00:05:24.723 --> 00:05:28.328
affects the visualisation without
changing the data themselves.

101
00:05:28.408 --> 00:05:31.677
For example,
too few bins hide some details,

102
00:05:31.757 --> 00:05:35.697
while too many can make
the graph harder to read.

103
00:05:35.777 --> 00:05:38.742
Here, it is still readable.
Refer back to video

104
00:05:38.822 --> 00:05:41.970
2 of session 2

105
00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:45.106
to review histograms
and the choice of bins.

106
00:05:45.186 --> 00:05:48.424
So the visualisation
needs careful thought.

107
00:05:48.504 --> 00:05:51.606
A histogram should give us a simple view

108
00:05:51.686 --> 00:05:55.277
of our data without having
to read the entire table.

109
00:05:56.681 --> 00:06:00.100
Third question:
how does nitrate concentration

110
00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:03.713
vary from one catchment to another?

111
00:06:03.793 --> 00:06:07.438
To explore this, we will focus on four

112
00:06:07.518 --> 00:06:11.107
catchments: North, East, South and West.

113
00:06:11.187 --> 00:06:14.719
We plot nitrate concentration
in milligrams per litre.

114
00:06:14.799 --> 00:06:16.921
Make sure you use the same

115
00:06:17.001 --> 00:06:19.597
scale so that the groups are comparable.

116
00:06:20.259 --> 00:06:23.515
Here, we combine a numerical variable,

117
00:06:23.595 --> 00:06:27.981
concentration,
with a categorical variable, catchment.

118
00:06:28.061 --> 00:06:31.425
We could draw one histogram per catchment.

119
00:06:31.505 --> 00:06:34.761
But comparing several shapes at once

120
00:06:34.841 --> 00:06:38.689
is difficult and can reduce readability.

121
00:06:38.769 --> 00:06:41.164
If we overlay all the histograms, they

122
00:06:41.244 --> 00:06:43.719
may obscure one another.

123
00:06:43.811 --> 00:06:46.986
Instead, we can use dot plots

124
00:06:47.066 --> 00:06:50.794
to show all the observations,
separated by catchment.

125
00:06:50.874 --> 00:06:54.172
We use the same x-axis for all

126
00:06:54.252 --> 00:06:57.875
groups and place the sets
of points at different heights.

127
00:06:58.375 --> 00:07:01.113
We can see the distribution of the data,

128
00:07:01.193 --> 00:07:04.260
but we would like a more compact summary

129
00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:07.359
of each distribution on the same scale.

130
00:07:07.439 --> 00:07:10.130
That is why, in the next video,
we will look at

131
00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:12.901
box-and-whisker plots, or boxplots.

132
00:07:12.981 --> 00:07:16.073
They provide a more
compact representation.

133
00:07:16.153 --> 00:07:18.187
For now,
remember that showing only the mean

134
00:07:18.267 --> 00:07:20.503
on a graph would not be enough.

135
00:07:20.583 --> 00:07:24.027
Boxplots show more information,
including the median

136
00:07:24.107 --> 00:07:28.587
and interquartile range,
which we will discuss later.

137
00:07:29.247 --> 00:07:32.348
Fourth question: do temperature

138
00:07:32.428 --> 00:07:36.086
and dissolved oxygen appear to be related?

139
00:07:36.166 --> 00:07:39.434
This time, both variables
are numerical and continuous.

140
00:07:39.514 --> 00:07:42.113
We can place temperature on the horizontal

141
00:07:42.193 --> 00:07:45.070
axis and oxygen on the vertical axis.

142
00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:49.646
Each pair of values becomes
a point on the graph.

143
00:07:49.726 --> 00:07:52.739
This is how we build a scatterplot.

144
00:07:52.831 --> 00:07:56.818
Here, the temperature is
5 °C and the dissolved

145
00:07:56.898 --> 00:08:00.957
oxygen concentration is 11.6 mg/L.
We place the point

146
00:08:01.037 --> 00:08:05.314
at 5 °C on the x-axis
and 11.6 mg/L on the y-axis.

147
00:08:05.394 --> 00:08:09.526
We repeat this for each pair of values

148
00:08:09.606 --> 00:08:14.181
to build the scatterplot.

149
00:08:14.673 --> 00:08:18.184
We then examine the direction,

150
00:08:18.264 --> 00:08:21.775
shape and strength of the association,

151
00:08:21.855 --> 00:08:25.697
as well as groups
and unusual observations.

152
00:08:25.777 --> 00:08:27.796
Be careful: seeing a slope

153
00:08:27.876 --> 00:08:30.227
on the graph is not enough to establish

154
00:08:30.307 --> 00:08:32.549
causality between the two variables.

155
00:08:32.629 --> 00:08:36.264
The relationship may depend on other
variables or a shared mechanism.

156
00:08:36.344 --> 00:08:39.979
We will discuss this in session six.

157
00:08:40.059 --> 00:08:43.281
For now, the graph helps us identify

158
00:08:43.361 --> 00:08:47.541
an overall pattern between
temperature and oxygen.

159
00:08:47.633 --> 00:08:51.517
Last question:
how does nitrate concentration

160
00:08:51.597 --> 00:08:55.602
change over time?

161
00:08:55.682 --> 00:09:00.347
We have a continuous variable
measured over time. We place

162
00:09:00.427 --> 00:09:04.791
time on the x-axis
and nitrate concentration

163
00:09:04.871 --> 00:09:10.037
on the y-axis,
with one point per observation.

164
00:09:10.785 --> 00:09:14.483
We then join the points
to make a line chart.

165
00:09:14.563 --> 00:09:18.704
The line is a visual guide, but it helps

166
00:09:18.784 --> 00:09:23.093
us assess how much the values
change between points.

167
00:09:23.173 --> 00:09:26.204
For example,
the change from February to March

168
00:09:26.284 --> 00:09:29.704
is greater than from January to February,
because

169
00:09:29.784 --> 00:09:33.426
the segment from February
to March has a steeper

170
00:09:33.506 --> 00:09:37.061
slope than the one from
January to February.

171
00:09:37.153 --> 00:09:39.632
This is purely visual, but the slope

172
00:09:39.712 --> 00:09:42.653
helps us notice these differences.

173
00:09:43.585 --> 00:09:47.210
Now that we have seen
several types of graphs,

174
00:09:47.290 --> 00:09:51.107
you can use this table
whenever you are unsure.

175
00:09:51.187 --> 00:09:53.417
It summarises the different situations.

176
00:09:53.497 --> 00:09:55.245
The research question

177
00:09:55.325 --> 00:09:57.844
guides the choice of graph,
not the other way round.

178
00:09:57.924 --> 00:10:01.404
Graphs help us understand data

179
00:10:01.484 --> 00:10:05.189
through positions, lengths and colours.

180
00:10:05.281 --> 00:10:07.519
We can also use sizes and shapes.

181
00:10:07.599 --> 00:10:10.368
Let us see how these features

182
00:10:10.448 --> 00:10:14.021
can make our graphs easier to read.

183
00:10:14.507 --> 00:10:17.858
Position on a common scale allows us,

184
00:10:17.938 --> 00:10:21.493
for example, to compare values precisely.

185
00:10:21.573 --> 00:10:25.026
Length works well
for representing quantities.

186
00:10:25.106 --> 00:10:28.611
Bars can sometimes make
quantities easier to judge

187
00:10:28.691 --> 00:10:32.451
than simple points on a line.

188
00:10:32.531 --> 00:10:36.017
Colour helps distinguish groups

189
00:10:36.109 --> 00:10:38.688
and represent ordered values.

190
00:10:38.768 --> 00:10:41.735
For nitrate concentration,
for example, we can

191
00:10:41.815 --> 00:10:44.726
define classes ranging from low

192
00:10:44.806 --> 00:10:47.994
to high values, and use colour
to distinguish their intensity.

193
00:10:48.074 --> 00:10:52.038
Colours can also make
a graph easier to read.

194
00:10:52.118 --> 00:10:55.473
With a set of points,
I cannot tell whether

195
00:10:55.553 --> 00:10:59.184
an observation comes from North,
East or another catchment.

196
00:10:59.264 --> 00:11:02.865
By adding colours and a legend,

197
00:11:02.957 --> 00:11:07.976
I can immediately identify
where each point comes from.

198
00:11:08.056 --> 00:11:10.641
The information becomes
much easier to see.

199
00:11:10.721 --> 00:11:15.869
However, do not rely on colour alone.

200
00:11:15.949 --> 00:11:18.955
Add shapes too, because colour perception

201
00:11:19.035 --> 00:11:21.760
varies between people. A graph

202
00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:24.785
that is readable for you
may not be for others.

203
00:11:24.877 --> 00:11:26.796
For example, we can use

204
00:11:26.876 --> 00:11:28.844
triangles, squares and other shapes.

205
00:11:28.924 --> 00:11:30.599
Although colour is useful,

206
00:11:30.679 --> 00:11:33.378
do not rely on it alone.

207
00:11:33.458 --> 00:11:36.449
Next, we can vary size.

208
00:11:36.529 --> 00:11:40.057
For 10 or 20 observations,
for example, we can use

209
00:11:40.137 --> 00:11:43.397
a circle whose size is
proportional to the count.

210
00:11:43.477 --> 00:11:47.127
This is useful, but precise comparisons

211
00:11:47.207 --> 00:11:50.745
are harder. Judging the length of a bar

212
00:11:50.825 --> 00:11:54.920
is easier than judging
the size of a circle:

213
00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:58.897
our perception of areas can be misleading.

214
00:11:58.989 --> 00:12:01.806
This gives us a sense of the quantities,

215
00:12:01.886 --> 00:12:05.009
but not a very precise comparison.

216
00:12:05.089 --> 00:12:08.312
Finally, we can use facets.

217
00:12:08.392 --> 00:12:09.888
What are facets?

218
00:12:09.968 --> 00:12:12.989
The same type of graph
is repeated for several

219
00:12:13.069 --> 00:12:16.271
categories: here, North,
East, South and West.

220
00:12:16.761 --> 00:12:20.528
Facets avoid putting all the points

221
00:12:20.608 --> 00:12:24.526
on one graph,
so each panel is less crowded.

222
00:12:24.606 --> 00:12:28.723
But with many categories,
they become harder to read.

223
00:12:28.803 --> 00:12:32.720
Here, four catchments give us
four easily comparable graphs.

224
00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:36.797
With many more graphs,
it would be more complicated.

225
00:12:37.017 --> 00:12:40.868
With facets, it is best to keep

226
00:12:40.948 --> 00:12:44.798
the same x- and y-axes.

227
00:12:44.878 --> 00:12:49.071
Otherwise, differences in scale
make comparisons difficult.

228
00:12:49.545 --> 00:12:52.144
Let us finish with some good practices

229
00:12:52.224 --> 00:12:55.181
for making a graph readable.

230
00:12:55.261 --> 00:12:59.170
At a minimum, we need a title, named axes,

231
00:12:59.250 --> 00:13:03.219
units where appropriate and tick marks.

232
00:13:03.299 --> 00:13:06.194
If colours, shapes

233
00:13:06.274 --> 00:13:09.714
or sizes carry information, add a legend.

234
00:13:09.794 --> 00:13:13.086
This takes us from the original
unlabelled graph to this one.

235
00:13:13.166 --> 00:13:16.557
These details are essential
for communicating information,

236
00:13:16.637 --> 00:13:20.653
particularly in a report or presentation.

237
00:13:20.745 --> 00:13:22.881
Without them, the reader cannot

238
00:13:22.961 --> 00:13:25.251
check what we are showing.

239
00:13:25.331 --> 00:13:27.569
So remember all these elements

240
00:13:27.649 --> 00:13:30.094
when building a graph.

241
00:13:30.174 --> 00:13:33.494
Graphs are not just for presentations.

242
00:13:33.574 --> 00:13:37.101
They are also useful
for exploratory analysis.

243
00:13:37.181 --> 00:13:38.492
What does that mean?

244
00:13:38.572 --> 00:13:41.996
This is an essential stage of the work.
You receive

245
00:13:42.076 --> 00:13:45.837
your raw data, then ask:

246
00:13:45.929 --> 00:13:48.246
“What can I study with these data?”

247
00:13:48.326 --> 00:13:51.818
Sometimes, we start with a good
research question, then

248
00:13:51.898 --> 00:13:55.390
realise that the data cannot answer it.

249
00:13:55.470 --> 00:13:58.303
Conversely,
after collecting the data, we may

250
00:13:58.383 --> 00:14:01.358
discover something particularly
interesting to investigate.

251
00:14:01.438 --> 00:14:04.037
We then adjust the research question

252
00:14:04.117 --> 00:14:07.501
to improve the study.

253
00:14:07.593 --> 00:14:11.106
This exploratory stage matters, and graphs

254
00:14:11.186 --> 00:14:14.651
can be very helpful. For example,

255
00:14:14.731 --> 00:14:18.433
the dataset may contain one
or two unusual observations.

256
00:14:18.513 --> 00:14:21.127
Reading the entire table
would take a long time.

257
00:14:21.207 --> 00:14:24.767
In a scatterplot or histogram,

258
00:14:24.847 --> 00:14:28.407
these observations stand out immediately.
We can then refine

259
00:14:28.487 --> 00:14:32.269
our research question to explore
what looks interesting.

260
00:14:32.879 --> 00:14:35.641
This kind of graph
raises several questions:

261
00:14:35.721 --> 00:14:38.831
“Are there groups or subgroups?

262
00:14:38.911 --> 00:14:42.072
Does the pattern follow
a straight line or a curve?

263
00:14:42.152 --> 00:14:45.262
Are there isolated values?”

264
00:14:45.342 --> 00:14:49.231
This helps us refine
our research question.

265
00:14:49.709 --> 00:14:52.355
To sum up, when choosing graphs,

266
00:14:52.435 --> 00:14:55.572
we always follow the same sequence.

267
00:14:55.652 --> 00:14:58.789
First,
we formulate the question and identify

268
00:14:58.869 --> 00:15:02.933
the variables needed to answer it.

269
00:15:03.013 --> 00:15:06.121
Then we choose the visual
task and type of graph,

270
00:15:06.601 --> 00:15:10.351
and check what could be misinterpreted.

271
00:15:10.803 --> 00:15:14.207
In the next video,
we will return to the question

272
00:15:14.287 --> 00:15:17.831
we set aside when I mentioned boxplots.

273
00:15:17.911 --> 00:15:20.562
How can we summarise
a distribution and compare

274
00:15:20.642 --> 00:15:23.528
groups without reducing them to their mean

275
00:15:23.608 --> 00:15:26.446
or median?

276
00:15:26.526 --> 00:15:29.318
As we have seen, these two indicators

277
00:15:29.398 --> 00:15:32.378
have limitations: they do not
describe the whole distribution.

278
00:15:32.458 --> 00:15:34.543
For the normal distribution,

279
00:15:34.623 --> 00:15:37.495
for example,
we also needed the standard deviation.

280
00:15:37.587 --> 00:15:40.145
So in the next video, we will learn how to

281
00:15:40.225 --> 00:15:43.111
build boxplots to summarise
our distributions.
